        "thinking": "Now I understand the situation. I need to add two more routes to the CF plugins module:\n\n1. **`GET /api/plugins/examples`** — The server uses `existsSync` to filter plugins by whether their local path exists. In CF Workers, there's no filesystem, so we can't do that. The fix: return the static list WITHOUT the `localPath` check. Since plugins are installed via npm in CF (no local paths), we should return the list but with `localPath` stripped (or set to null) for all examples, since users can still install them via npm package name.\n\nActually looking at what `listBundledPluginExamples()` does: it returns the BUNDLED_PLUGIN_EXAMPLES but only those where `existsSync(absoluteLocalPath)` is true. \n\nIn CF production, the filesystem checks would filter out all the examples since local paths don't exist there, but from a UX perspective users should still see them as installable via npm. So I'll return all examples with `localPath` set to `null` instead of trying to probe the filesystem.\n\n2. **`POST /api/plugins/install`** — This is more complex. It uses `loader.installPlugin()` which runs `npm install` and loads...\n\nSetting up the sidecar proxy...\n\nThe sidecar is a Node.js companion server that exposes the same API endpoints, so I can proxy the install request there. For the examples endpoint, I'll return a static list without filesystem checks and omit local paths since CF Workers can't access the filesystem. When the sidecar isn't configured, I'll return a clear error message. For the examples endpoint in a CF Workers environment, there's no filesystem access, so I need to decide whether to strip out the `localPath` field entirely or return it as null. Let me check how the AvailablePluginExample type actually uses this field to determine the best approach.",
